# mgcrom.msg - Confessor Cromwell of the Children of Atom (mgcrom.ssl).
# 100-105 look, 110-141 the sermons (six lines each, spoken in order), 150-156 the night vigil, 200+ dialogue.
# One entry per line; no braces in comments.

{100}{}{You see a gaunt man in stained robes, standing ankle-deep in the glowing pool.}
{101}{}{You see Confessor Cromwell, standing in his god's bathwater.}
{102}{}{His robes end in a green tide-mark. He has no eyebrows, very few teeth, and the calm of a man who has seen the end of the world and liked it.}
{103}{}{His hands are trembling. He keeps glancing at the bomb the way a man looks at a dog that has started to growl.}
{105}{}{I will pray for you. From a distance.}

# 110-115: the bomb is dormant.
{110}{}{Gather at the water, children! Our iron guest has kept it warm for another morning!}
{111}{}{A speck of dust holds room for worlds! Think what room there must be in a sinner!}
{112}{}{When Division comes, no soul will leave empty-handed. Even the poorest shall have worlds to tend!}
{113}{}{Bring your doubts into the Glow! If you lose a tooth, leave that as well!}
{114}{}{The old world spent its fire on borders. Atom has greater work for it!}
{115}{}{When our iron guest rises, there will be light enough for every child! Until then, keep clear of Walter's valves!}

# 120-125: the bomb is disarmed.
{120}{}{Atom sleeps! They have closed His eye with a wrench, and still He is Atom!}
{121}{}{Do not grieve, children. A wire is not a soul. No tool unmakes what is in every grain of dust.}
{122}{}{They say He is safe now. He was never unsafe. He was patient.}
{123}{}{The Glow is still in the water. Stand in it, and tell me He is gone!}
{124}{}{Forgive the hands that silenced Him. They knew not what they tightened.}
{125}{}{Atom sleeps deeper tonight. We shall sing softer.}

# 130-135: Burke's charge is fitted.
{130}{}{Atom stirs! Did you feel it? The water shivered!}
{131}{}{He hums, children! A new note! Twenty years I have stood here; I know His voice!}
{132}{}{Something has touched Him. Something that was not prayer.}
{133}{}{Be ready. Wash. Settle your debts. The Division may come before the harvest.}
{134}{}{I asked for a sign. I did not ask for it this week.}
{135}{}{Atom stirs, and I am glad. Of course I am glad.}

# 140-141: Simms is dead.
{140}{}{Lucas Simms is divided now, and every part of him keeps the peace in a world of its own.}
{141}{}{Pray for the sheriff. He never knelt here, but he never spat here either.}

# 150-156: the night vigil (22:00 to 06:00). 155 only with the charge fitted, 156 only once disarmed.
{150}{}{...and keep the wall, and keep the well, and keep those who sleep beside You...}
{151}{}{Sleep, town. He and I will keep the watch.}
{152}{}{Twenty years of nights, and He has never once answered. I admire that in a god.}
{153}{}{The water is warmest after midnight. Nobody believes me. Nobody is awake to try it.}
{154}{}{Still here, Lord. So am I. Good night.}
{155}{}{He stirs even at night now. Neither of us is sleeping.}
{156}{}{Quiet tonight. Quieter than quiet. I had not known there was such a thing.}

# Greetings.
{200}{}{Come closer, traveler. The water is warm, and it has waited a long time for you. I am Cromwell, Confessor to the Children of Atom. He sleeps beside me. Speak softly, or don't; He has slept through worse.}
{201}{}{The Glow be upon you, child. You have come back to the water. They always do. It is the one warm place in town.}
{202}{}{Atom sleeps, child, and I keep His bed. Did you come to see the grave you dug? There is none. Wade in and feel for yourself.}
{203}{}{Atom stirs. I have waited my whole life to say those words, and I find I am saying them very quietly. What do you want, child?}
{204}{}{You have heard. Lucas Simms is divided, as we all shall be. He would have hated to hear me say it. I say it fondly.}
{205}{}{Ask, child. I have all the time there is. Standing in water teaches patience.}
{206}{}{So you are the one. Come closer; I don't bite, and He no longer can. I am Cromwell, Confessor to the Children of Atom. You have met my god. You left Him sleeping.}
{207}{}{Atom sleeps, and we wait. You are still welcome at the water, child. It holds no grudges. It holds very little, these days.}
{208}{}{I have other questions.}

# Hub options.
{210}{}{What is this church of yours?}
{211}{}{You are standing in radioactive water.}
{212}{}{I'd like to give something to your church.}
{213}{}{The sheriff wants this bomb disarmed.}
{214}{}{What if somebody woke Atom?}
{215}{}{I have something to confess. It concerns the bomb.}
{216}{}{I am the one who disarmed your god.}
{217}{}{Water glow pretty.}
{218}{}{I'll leave you to your flock.}
{219}{}{Bye, glow man.}

# The creed.
{220}{}{We are the Children of Atom. In every atom there is a universe entire. When He wakes, each of us is divided, and from one tired sinner come worlds without number. It is not death, child. It is arithmetic.}
{221}{}{And the bomb?}
{222}{}{The bomb is Atom, asleep in iron. Men built Him to end a war, and thought that was all He was for. People are often wrong about what a god is for. He forgives them. So far.}
{223}{}{The bomb was Atom's cradle, and now it is only iron. But He is in the water, and in your bones, and in the dust on your boots. A wrench reaches none of those. Believe me, the sheriff has asked.}

# The water.
{230}{}{I am bathed in the Glow, child. Twenty years, every day. My hair went first, then my toenails, and I have never felt nearer to Him. The doctor has another name for it. The doctor is not a happy man.}
{231}{}{Doesn't it hurt?}
{232}{}{Only the teeth. And each year there are fewer teeth to hurt. Atom provides.}

# Donations.
{240}{}{Atom needs nothing. His Children need candles, soup and a roof that keeps out most of the rain. Give what your heart allows; He will know the rest.}
{241}{}{Ten dollars.}
{242}{}{Fifty dollars.}
{243}{}{A hundred dollars.}
{244}{}{On second thought, no.}
{245}{}{Then keep it, with His blessing. He is patient. You may have noticed.}
{246}{}{I have nothing to spare.}
{250}{}{Ten dollars of candles. Every flame a small division. Bless you, child.}
{251}{}{Fifty! The roof over the altar thanks you, and so does everyone who kneels beneath the drip.}
{252}{}{A hundred dollars. For this I would baptize you myself, here and now. No? The offer stands. So do I, most days, to the ankles.}
{254}{}{No more, child. You have given enough; Atom keeps no ledger past a certain sum. Mother Maya does, and even she is content. Spend the rest on boots.}

# The sheriff's plan.
{260}{}{Lucas has wanted that since the day he pinned on the star. We do not approve. We will also not lift a hand. Take your wrench to Him. You can no more unmake Atom than unscrew the sun.}
{261}{}{So you won't stop me.}
{262}{}{Stop you? I shall pray for you, child. Loudly, from right here, the whole time you work. You may find it distracting.}

# Sounding him out about a detonation.
{270}{}{Woke... you say it the way a man asks the price of a mule. No. Not by a stranger's hand, and never for coin. The Division is a gift, and a gift that is sold is only a theft. Who has been talking to you?}
{271}{}{Nobody. An idle thought.}
{272}{}{Then leave it idle. Idle things do the least harm. Look at me.}
{273}{}{A man in a suit, at the saloon.}
{274}{}{Then go to Lucas Simms. I say this as a man who has never once agreed with the sheriff: tell him today. I preach the end of the world. I do not rent it out.}

# The confession.
{280}{}{You. You put a stranger's key in His side, for money. Twenty years I have prayed for the Division, and I tell you: not like this. Not bought. Take it out. If your hands shake, tell the sheriff first. Then take it out.}
{281}{}{I'll undo it.}
{282}{}{Then go, and quickly. I will keep the water warm. I always do.}
{283}{}{It's done. Make your peace.}
{284}{}{My peace was made long ago. See to your own, child. You have less time than you think, and more than you have earned.}
{285}{}{[Leave him to the water.]}

# After the disarming.
{290}{}{I know. I heard Him stop humming; I had never noticed the hum until it ended. You meant well. They always mean well. But sleeping is not dying, child. We will wait. We are very good at waiting.}
{291}{}{No hard feelings, then?}
{292}{}{I have many feelings, and some of them are hard. But none of them will follow you home with a knife. That is the difference between us and most churches.}

# Low Intelligence.
{300}{}{Pretty! Yes! You see truer than the clever ones. Kneel, child. There. The Glow be upon you, now and at the Division.}
{301}{}{Cromwell pours a handful of warm water over your head. It tingles all the way down.}
{302}{}{Head warm. Tingly!}
